{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Closed the last sampling limitation of the split-brain chain by extending the healed phase into a long run. The two-mode cluster harness now commits 200 further entries on the healed leader, waits until that leader's log has advanced at least 150 entries past the conflict index, and only then samples, reporting how many entries followed the conflict. Local construction feedback over both restart modes shows the window is substantial (202 and 212 entries after healing) and that the feature-on state is unchanged by it: the restarted node still reports {A,B,C}, the entry at the conflict index is still inconsistent across nodes, and its FSM still holds the minority write, while the feature-off control run stays consistent and reports {A,B,C,D,E}. The successor experiment is a revision of the child question's previous experiment (same investigation) with mode-conditioned admissions that avoid pinning schedule-dependent indexes and six comparisons over the healed event: entry consistency and configuration after the long run in the feature-on mode (expected violated), the long-run and progress operands, and the same consistency and configuration predicates in the control mode (expected holds). Incomplete: that receipt and its interpretation, and the user Restore and term-changing truncation repair paths for the repair question.\"}"}}
